Question: If i want to include an interactive computer vision based interactive website, which python or non python libraries would be needed to downloaded. Or can I just use JavaScript

Response:

Yes, you can do it entirely in JavaScript, with nothing to install. Modern CV libraries run in the browser using WebAssembly/WebGL, and the webcam is a built-in browser API. Python is only needed if your assignment requires it or you want to run heavy models on a server.

## JavaScript only (runs in the browser)

You load these from a CDN with a <script> tag, or from npm if you're using a bundler:

- MediaPipe Tasks Vision (@mediapipe/tasks-vision): hand tracking, face landmarks, pose, object detection, segmentation. It's the easiest way to build real-time interactive things like gesture controls or face filters.
- TensorFlow.js (@tensorflow/tfjs) and its prebuilt models (coco-ssd, pose-detection, face-landmarks-detection).
- OpenCV.js: classic CV like edge detection, contours, color filtering and blurring.
- ONNX Runtime Web or Transformers.js: for running custom models in the browser, such as a YOLO model exported to ONNX or Hugging Face models.
- ml5.js + p5.js: beginner-friendly wrappers, good for creative or visual projects.
- Built-in browser APIs: navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia() for the webcam, and <canvas> for drawing overlays.

One catch: browsers only allow webcam access over HTTPS or localhost. Opening the HTML file directly (file://) may block the camera. To test locally, run a simple server from your project folder:

    python3 -m http.server 8000

Then open http://localhost:8000. GitHub Pages also works for hosting, since it uses HTTPS.

## If you need Python

- Python-only UI (quickest): gradio or streamlit with streamlit-webrtc, plus opencv-python and mediapipe. You get a web page without writing any JS.
- Custom backend: flask or fastapi plus opencv-python, with ultralytics/torch if you need them. The browser sends frames to the server (by upload or WebSocket). This takes more work and adds lag, so it's only worth it for models too heavy for the browser.

## Which to pick

For a webcam-based interactive site, plain HTML/JS with MediaPipe is the simplest. You install nothing, it runs in real time, and it's easy to host. Your repo has a main.py placeholder, so if the assignment requires Python, Gradio or Streamlit is the easiest way to meet that. I haven't seen the assignment requirements, so check those first.

